  "Question": "The line \\(y=-x+3\\) intersects the x-axis at \\((3,0)\\), and \\(y\\) decreases as \\(x\\) increases. The solution set of the inequality \\(-x+3<0\\) is ( ).",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x<3\\)",
    "B": "\\(x>-3\\)",
    "C": "\\(x>3\\)",
    "D": "\\(x<-3\\)"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "\\(y<0\\) corresponds to the graph being below the x-axis. Since \\(y\\) decreases as \\(x\\) increases, this occurs to the right of the intersection, that is, \\(x>3\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Solve using the negative coefficient\n\nTo isolate \\(x\\), subtract \\(3\\) from both sides of \\(-x+3<0\\), giving \\(-x<-3\\). Dividing both sides by \\(-1\\) reverses the inequality. Therefore the solution set is \\(x>3\\), with \\(x=3\\) excluded, choice C.",
